{:edb/db-before-t 2327 :edb/db-after-t 2328 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535640 50 #inst "2026-10-09T04:18:26.956Z" 13194139535640 true] [17592186105575 1100 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n\n- The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n\n- The number of months in a particular year" 13194139535640 true] [17592186105575 1100 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n1. The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped $6$ times\n2. The number of times a $6$-sided die is rolled until a $6$ appears\n3. The number of months in a particular year" 13194139535640 false] [17592186105575 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6.$\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535640 true] [17592186105575 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: $x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6$.\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,\\ldots$\n- Statement III does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of months in a particular year is not the outcome of a trial or experiment. (By definition, there are $12$ months in a year.)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535640 false] [17592186105738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358936 13194139535640 true] [17592186105738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358943 13194139535640 true] [17592186105738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358950 13194139535640 true] [17592186105738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186358957 13194139535640 true] [17592186358936 1017 #edb/ref 17592186358937 13194139535640 true] [17592186358936 1089 "q-125075" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358936 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535640 true] [17592186358936 1100 "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The time it takes for a student to complete math homework\n- The number of hours you spend jogging in a week\n- The number of candies in a jar" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358936 1113 #uuid "e47187ec-f899-5cff-85d8-51e83161b9ff" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358936 1145 "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358938 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358939 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358940 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358941 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1000 #edb/ref 17592186358942 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1041 "selection" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1104 #edb/ref 17592186358938 13194139535640 true] [17592186358937 1110 #uuid "f79d7e0b-2b15-5fa9-9960-242560c81a1d" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358938 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358938 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535640 true] [17592186358938 1111 #uuid "315a3560-94c3-5a3d-8a88-b6b6b093ae82" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358939 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358939 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535640 true] [17592186358939 1111 #uuid "6d98f302-d61f-5d4f-b443-174e9ac52b0b" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358940 1034 "I only" 13194139535640 true] [17592186358940 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535640 true] [17592186358940 1111 #uuid "161d95cf-2985-5e9f-9993-fbe1089d2210" 13194139535640 true] [1759218